 me  that's what i'm feeling the the two  words to describe it it's just  i'm just tired of the floods more than  30 000 people have been evacuated from  homes in and around sydney  or have been warned to get ready for it  as yet again australia's east coast is  battered by rain  a month's worth of it  in only two days it's incredible uh in  the last  18 months we've had three major flood  events in this area and we're talking  about water depths getting way above  head level in some areas and you know  multiple people having to be evacuated  animals needing to be rescued and also  people as well  wild weather saw a cargo ship stuck  without power  and drifting towards rocky sea cliffs  a helicopter rescue had to be aborted  eventually it was towed away from danger  this major bridge on the outskirts of  sydney has been lost under a mile wide  river and with the flood waters still  rising people are bracing themselves for  even more homes to go under  so they're getting ready  moving whatever they can to higher  ground or a shipping container  this is an area prone to flooding  but not this often  keith levy moved in here six months ago  since then  four floods  the ground floor is under a meter of  muddy water  he's given up bothering to pump it out  is this climate change  i think partially i think there's a lot  of factors i think damn management as  well is um  the cause of what's also going on and  also the human footprint we're putting  on  the area as well  with barely time to recover from one  flood the region is hit with another  it's been called sydney's new normal  but nothing feels normal about this  nicole johnston sky news  sydney  more than a dozen people are still  missing after a huge chunk of glacier  broke off in the italian alps yesterday  several people are reported to have been  killed when the ice fell from the  marmalade glacier unleashing a huge  avalanche of ice snow and rocks now  experts are pointing to climate change  as a calls for the 